    "summary": "High‑energy Bertha opener and double Cryptical/Other One suite shines",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a powerful Bertha start and an expansive The Other One suite bookended by two Cryptical Envelopments. Comes A Time lands beautifully after the marathon jam, keeping the energy high throughout the second set. Despite intermittent piano distortion, the performance is compelling and worth hearing.",
    "review": "November 6, 1971 at San Francisco's Harding Theater finds the Dead locked in a tight groove that carries from the opening Bertha straight into a rolling tide of classics. Jerry’s guitar rides confidently over a solid rhythm foundation while Phil anchors the long improvisational stretch with his characteristic melodic bass lines. The centerpiece is the double‑Cryptical The Other One suite – two Cryptical Envelopments framing an extended The Other One that builds tension before resolving into a pristine Comes A Time, a song choice many felt was especially apt for the evening. The second set continues with Sugar Magnolia and a seamless Not Fade Away/Feel Good jam, maintaining momentum despite Keith’s piano suffering intermittent distortion. Overall, the soundboard capture, while not flawless, preserves the band’s high‑energy interplay and makes this show a standout snapshot of the early‑’70s era.",
